WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.050
For billions of years before your birth you did not exist, and that fact has never once troubled you.

2
00:00:06.050 --> 00:00:11.460
So why does the very same nonexistence, when it lies ahead, keep you awake at night?

3
00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:15.750
Perhaps what we fear is not death, but the life we have not yet lived.

4
00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:28.560
One night, when sleep wouldn't come, did you ever think about your own death?

5
00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:30.290
Most of us have.

6
00:00:30.290 --> 00:00:38.580
When that thought arrives there's a small tightening inside the ribcage, the mind bolts somewhere else, you reach for your phone.

7
00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:43.990
Now perform the same mental movement in reverse: think of the year eighteen fifty.

8
00:00:43.990 --> 00:00:46.040
You weren't there.

9
00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:50.010
Think of sixteen fifty — again, you weren't there.

10
00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:59.420
While the pyramids were rising in Egypt, while armies faced each other at Manzikert, while Ottoman envoys made their way along the roads to Vienna, you did not exist.

11
00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:03.870
For billions of years a universe turned and turned without you.

12
00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:09.440
Now put your hand on your heart and be honest: this doesn't trouble you at all, not in the least.

13
00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:12.610
It hasn't cost you a single night's sleep.

14
00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:17.540
And here stands one of the most provocative questions in the history of philosophy.

15
00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:24.070
If the nothingness after death fills you with dread, why doesn't the nothingness before birth do the same?

16
00:01:24.070 --> 00:01:26.120
Aren't they the same thing?

17
00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:30.090
Both of them are an infinite stretch of time in which you are not.

18
00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:37.420
The first man to ask this question systematically was a philosopher born on the island of Samos who bought a garden in Athens.

19
00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:42.670
Epicurus lived between three hundred forty-one and two hundred seventy before the common era.

20
00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:48.560
In that garden he purchased outside the city walls, he founded the school that still bears his name.

21
00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:54.290
Women were admitted there, and so were slaves — something quite extraordinary for the period.

22
00:01:54.290 --> 00:02:02.260
What he taught was a kind of prescription for mental health: what unsettles a person is not the world itself, but their false beliefs about the world.

23
00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:06.550
And the most poisonous of all false beliefs was the fear of death.

24
00:02:06.550 --> 00:02:12.600
In a letter to a student named Menoeceus, he builds his argument with an almost mathematical brevity.

25
00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:17.050
Whether something is good or bad for us depends on our being able to feel it.

26
00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:19.740
A toothache is bad because you feel it.

27
00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:23.550
Death, however, is the complete cessation of feeling.

28
00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:27.680
Therefore death is neither bad nor good; death is nothing.

29
00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:35.650
And from this comes that famous line: while we exist, death is not; when death comes, we are not.

30
00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:38.180
The two are never in the same room.

31
00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:40.870
You will never meet the thing you fear.

32
00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:46.120
On paper this argument looks flawless, and in practice it comforts almost no one.

33
00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:51.530
People hear it, they nod, and then at night they feel the same tightening all over again.

34
00:02:51.530 --> 00:03:00.460
Epicurus's followers must have noticed this too, because two and a half centuries later a Roman poet rebuilt the same idea in a far more insidious form.

35
00:03:00.460 --> 00:03:11.150
Lucretius lived roughly between ninety-nine and fifty-five before the common era, and he wrote a colossal poem in six books: De Rerum Natura, that is, On the Nature of Things.

36
00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:16.080
The third book of this poem is a reckoning with the fear of death from beginning to end.

37
00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:23.410
And there, in the pages of On the Nature of Things, he makes a move that is still argued over in philosophy classes today.

38
00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:28.020
He says: nature is holding a mirror up to you — look into it.

39
00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:32.150
You shudder when you think of the infinite time that follows your death.

40
00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:35.480
Well then, look at the infinite time before your birth.

41
00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:37.850
It was exactly the same.

42
00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:47.740
The example he chose was the kind to raise the hair on a Roman reader's neck: while Carthage and Rome were at war, while Hannibal crossed the Alps with elephants and ravaged Italy,

43
00:03:47.740 --> 00:03:52.510
while everyone wondered which empire would be left standing — you were not there.

44
00:03:52.510 --> 00:03:57.760
The world shook, cities burned, tens of thousands died.

45
00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:00.770
You feel no sorrow at having missed any of it.

46
00:04:00.770 --> 00:04:03.620
Because there was no you to miss it.

47
00:04:03.620 --> 00:04:09.990
On the gravestones of Rome, a four-letter abbreviation of this very thought was even in circulation.

48
00:04:09.990 --> 00:04:14.760
It stood for: I was not, I was, I am not, I do not care.

49
00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:20.010
That must be the coolest-headed sentence a person could have carved onto their own tombstone.

50
00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:22.860
This is the symmetry argument.

51
00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:25.070
Its structure is simple.

52
00:04:25.070 --> 00:04:30.000
The nothingness after death is the same kind of thing as the nothingness before birth.

53
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:34.930
The nothingness before birth is not bad — you already prove this by the way you behave.

54
00:04:34.930 --> 00:04:38.580
Therefore the nothingness after death is not bad either.

55
00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:43.510
Your fear is a miscalculation, the product of habit rather than logic.

56
00:04:43.510 --> 00:04:48.440
And you don't need a philosophy book to see how solid the ground beneath this is in everyday life.

57
00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:50.970
Open the old photo album at home.

58
00:04:50.970 --> 00:04:57.500
There's probably a picture of your mother or your father in their twenties, by the sea, hair in the wind, laughing.

59
00:04:57.500 --> 00:05:00.350
In that frame, you are absent.

60
00:05:00.350 --> 00:05:06.560
That summer happened, that laugh rang out, that evening ended, and you were nowhere to be found.

61
00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:09.570
What is it you feel looking at that photograph?

62
00:05:09.570 --> 00:05:14.340
Curiosity, perhaps a sweet kind of envy, perhaps tenderness.

63
00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:16.230
But not dread.

64
00:05:16.230 --> 00:05:22.280
Nobody looks at a photograph of their parent's youth and weeps, "I am devastated that I wasn't there."

65
00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:26.250
So why doesn't an argument this clean convince any of us?

66
00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:33.900
Vladimir Nabokov, in the very first pages of his autobiography Speak, Memory, tells of a young man he once knew.

67
00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:39.790
This young man had watched an amateur home movie shot in his family's house a few weeks before he was born.

68
00:05:39.790 --> 00:05:46.960
Everything in the footage is familiar: the same house, the same garden, the same people, the same staircase.

69
00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:49.970
His mother is waving from a window.

70
00:05:49.970 --> 00:05:51.860
Her belly is flat.

71
00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:57.750
And in one corner of the room stands a brand-new baby carriage, not yet used.

72
00:05:57.750 --> 00:06:05.400
The young man looks at that carriage and a chill runs through him, because an empty baby carriage strikes him, strangely, as a coffin.

73
00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:12.730
What truly shakes him is this: the world is there, complete, working perfectly, and his absence disrupts nothing.

74
00:06:12.730 --> 00:06:15.100
Nobody is looking for him.

75
00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:18.750
Nobody's face carries the trace of something missing.

76
00:06:18.750 --> 00:06:24.000
Nabokov calls this feeling chronophobia — the fear of time.

77
00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:34.210
And it is precisely here that he places the most quoted image in Speak, Memory: common sense tells us that human life is a brief crack of light between two eternities of darkness.

78
00:06:34.210 --> 00:06:38.340
We generally fear the darkness ahead of us, not the one behind.

79
00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:41.510
And yet the two are twins.

80
00:06:41.510 --> 00:06:51.880
This single example shows why the symmetry argument fails on an emotional level: the man does not refute the logic, he simply feels the same shudder when he looks backward too.

81
00:06:51.880 --> 00:06:56.490
So the symmetry is there, but our feeling is not symmetrical.

82
00:06:56.490 --> 00:07:02.060
Now, is this emotional asymmetry merely a flaw, or is there a reason behind it?

83
00:07:02.060 --> 00:07:06.190
One of the clearest minds of the twentieth century attempted an answer.

84
00:07:06.190 --> 00:07:14.960
Thomas Nagel, in a short essay published in nineteen seventy and later collected in his book Mortal Questions, rethought the question of why death is bad.

85
00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:19.090
Nagel's answer is this: death is not bad because it hurts.

86
00:07:19.090 --> 00:07:23.060
It is bad because it deprives you of something.

87
00:07:23.060 --> 00:07:25.910
This is called the deprivation account.

88
00:07:25.910 --> 00:07:30.200
To die is to have a life not yet lived taken out of your hands.

89
00:07:30.200 --> 00:07:37.210
The tragedy of someone who dies in an accident at twenty is not that the person suffers — very often there is no suffering at all.

90
00:07:37.210 --> 00:07:47.900
The tragedy lies in letters that will never be written, in a home that will never be built, in a craft that will never be learned, in children who will never be born, in people who will never be met.

91
00:07:47.900 --> 00:07:52.510
The loss is not inside experience; it is in the absence of experience.

92
00:07:52.510 --> 00:07:56.480
Nagel does not stop there, and he comes straight to our question.

93
00:07:56.480 --> 00:08:02.050
If death is a deprivation, then shouldn't the nothingness before birth be a deprivation as well?

94
00:08:02.050 --> 00:08:04.740
His answer is subtle.

95
00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:08.870
He says it is easy to imagine a person having lived longer.

96
00:08:08.870 --> 00:08:14.920
The same person, the same memories, the same bonds, just a few more years.

97
00:08:14.920 --> 00:08:19.530
But imagining a person having been born much earlier is not the same thing.

98
00:08:19.530 --> 00:08:24.940
Had you been born a hundred years earlier, that person would not have been you.

99
00:08:24.940 --> 00:08:31.790
Out of another body, another era, another language, an entirely different human being would have emerged.

100
00:08:31.790 --> 00:08:36.080
So the time after death is time that can be taken from you.

101
00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:41.810
The time before birth was time that could never, under any circumstances, have been yours.

102
00:08:41.810 --> 00:08:43.860
Is that convincing?

103
00:08:43.860 --> 00:08:46.550
It is still debated.

104
00:08:46.550 --> 00:08:51.480
But there is another layer to the matter, and the person who opened it was Derek Parfit.

105
00:08:51.480 --> 00:08:59.290
Parfit's nineteen eighty-four book Reasons and Persons contains a thought experiment showing that human beings are not neutral with respect to time.

106
00:08:59.290 --> 00:09:02.780
It goes like this: you wake up in a hospital.

107
00:09:02.780 --> 00:09:07.870
The last day of your memory has been erased with a drug — a standard procedure.

108
00:09:07.870 --> 00:09:10.400
You ask the nurse.

109
00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:13.890
She says there are two kinds of patient in this ward.

110
00:09:13.890 --> 00:09:21.380
Some of them underwent a horrific procedure yesterday that lasted ten hours with no painkillers; but they do not remember it.

111
00:09:21.380 --> 00:09:27.110
The others will undergo the same kind of procedure this afternoon, but it will last only one hour.

112
00:09:27.110 --> 00:09:31.720
To find out which one you are, she has to go and check your file.

113
00:09:31.720 --> 00:09:36.170
While the nurse goes off to the file, what is running through your mind?

114
00:09:36.170 --> 00:09:40.940
Almost everyone wants the same thing: please let it be yesterday's.

115
00:09:40.940 --> 00:09:46.830
Ten hours of horror is preferable to one hour of horror, as long as it is already behind you.

116
00:09:46.830 --> 00:09:52.240
And yet, in terms of the total quantity of pain, this is completely irrational.

117
00:09:52.240 --> 00:09:56.530
Parfit calls it bias toward the future.

118
00:09:56.530 --> 00:10:04.340
We are not creatures spread evenly through time; we live facing forward, and we treat the past like a debt already paid.

119
00:10:04.340 --> 00:10:09.910
And here, perhaps, is the real reason we do not grieve over the nothingness before our birth.

120
00:10:09.910 --> 00:10:13.880
Not a triumph of logic, but a habit of psychology.

121
00:10:13.880 --> 00:10:22.170
We do not grieve for the nothingness in the past because it is finished; we fear the nothingness in the future because it is still on its way.

122
00:10:22.170 --> 00:10:29.340
And that produces a conclusion with a certain chill to it: Lucretius may be right, but being right is not enough.

123
00:10:29.340 --> 00:10:32.510
Because fear is not uprooted by an argument.

124
00:10:32.510 --> 00:10:36.160
It is uprooted only by a way of living.

125
00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:41.730
At this point it is worth turning to an entirely different geography and an entirely different method.

126
00:10:41.730 --> 00:10:52.260
Because while at one end of the Mediterranean the advice was to tear the thought of death out of the mind, in the east the advice was exactly the opposite: to place death at the very centre of the mind.

127
00:10:52.260 --> 00:10:59.590
Al-Ghazali was born in ten fifty-eight in Tus, in what is now Iran, and died there in eleven eleven.

128
00:10:59.590 --> 00:11:07.560
He was the most brilliant teacher at the Nizamiyya Madrasa in Baghdad; in his thirties he occupied the most prestigious chair in the Islamic world.

129
00:11:07.560 --> 00:11:19.530
Then one day he left it all behind — the teaching and the title — and wandered for years, passing through a kind of spiritual crisis, and when he returned he wrote a colossal work: the Ihya Ulum al-Din,

130
00:11:19.530 --> 00:11:23.020
The Revival of the Religious Sciences.

131
00:11:23.020 --> 00:11:25.550
This book has forty chapters.

132
00:11:25.550 --> 00:11:33.680
And the very last chapter of The Revival of the Religious Sciences, the fortieth, is devoted from beginning to end to death and what comes after it.

133
00:11:33.680 --> 00:11:40.210
There, al-Ghazali recommends to his reader a habit that may strike them as strange: remember death often.

134
00:11:40.210 --> 00:11:43.220
Remember it several times a day.

135
00:11:43.220 --> 00:11:47.990
Go to funerals, go to graveyards, and stand there a long while.

136
00:11:47.990 --> 00:11:51.640
At first glance this looks like a method of torture.

137
00:11:51.640 --> 00:11:59.130
But al-Ghazali's reasoning runs like this: what rivets a person to this world is the silent assumption that death will never come.

138
00:11:59.130 --> 00:12:08.220
Once that assumption is broken, the weight of the things you have accumulated grows lighter, your resentments shrink, and you struggle to remember who it was you were angry with.

139
00:12:08.220 --> 00:12:14.430
Remembering death is not meant to darken life, but to sift out which part of life truly matters.

140
00:12:14.430 --> 00:12:17.600
Now set the two prescriptions side by side.

141
00:12:17.600 --> 00:12:21.730
One says: do not think about death, because it is nothing.

142
00:12:21.730 --> 00:12:26.500
The other says: think about death constantly, because it is a threshold.

143
00:12:26.500 --> 00:12:34.950
The methods are diametrically opposed, yet the place they arrive at is strikingly similar: taking life back out of the hands of fear.

144
00:12:34.950 --> 00:12:41.160
The question of non-existence before birth appears in this tradition too, in an entirely different key.

145
00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:48.970
In the Quran, the twenty-eighth verse of the chapter of the Cow addresses the human being like this: you were dead, and He gave you life.

146
00:12:48.970 --> 00:12:57.420
That is, your starting point was already non-existence; your existing right now is not a rule that must be preserved, but something given.

147
00:12:57.420 --> 00:13:08.270
The first verse of the chapter of Man looks into that same emptiness even more directly, and asks: has there not passed over the human being a long stretch of time when they were not yet a thing worth mentioning?

148
00:13:08.270 --> 00:13:12.400
This sentence is the very emptiness Lucretius was looking into.

149
00:13:12.400 --> 00:13:15.090
The same infinite darkness.

150
00:13:15.090 --> 00:13:21.140
But the tone of voice is entirely different; it is spoken not with a shudder, but with serenity.

151
00:13:21.140 --> 00:13:28.150
There, non-existence is not a catastrophe but a reminder: you are not necessary, you are on loan.

152
00:13:28.150 --> 00:13:34.200
And when this thought turns into poetry in Anatolia, it takes on its most human form.

153
00:13:34.200 --> 00:13:44.410
Yunus Emre lived in the second half of the thirteenth century and the beginning of the fourteenth, and he wrote the plainest lines about death that still circulate in Turkish today.

154
00:13:44.410 --> 00:13:50.940
Yunus does not fear death; he separates the body from the soul, and says bodies die but souls do not.

155
00:13:50.940 --> 00:13:55.070
Yet the same man also says there is one thing he cannot bear.

156
00:13:55.070 --> 00:14:03.360
One thing in this world burns him inside: those who die while still in their prime, like a field of grain cut down while it is still green.

157
00:14:03.360 --> 00:14:09.890
Those two lines are, in truth, Nagel's deprivation account written as poetry seven centuries earlier.

158
00:14:09.890 --> 00:14:15.940
Yunus is not objecting to death itself; he is objecting to being harvested before ripening.

159
00:14:15.940 --> 00:14:19.430
Grain cut in the field while it is still green.

160
00:14:19.430 --> 00:14:26.120
It shows exactly where the loss lies: not in any pain the dead person feels, but in what was never lived.

161
00:14:26.120 --> 00:14:29.930
And here there is a quiet observation about everyday life.

162
00:14:29.930 --> 00:14:33.100
Until a century ago, people died at home.

163
00:14:33.100 --> 00:14:42.510
Children saw the funeral, went to pay their condolences; the women of the neighbourhood washed the body; for three days the whole street revolved around that house.

164
00:14:42.510 --> 00:14:46.640
Death was inside life, somewhere between the kitchen and the bedroom.

165
00:14:46.640 --> 00:14:54.130
Today death mostly takes place at the end of a hospital corridor, in a room we do not know, in the hands of specialists.

166
00:14:54.130 --> 00:14:56.820
We have removed it from our lives.

167
00:14:56.820 --> 00:15:03.030
But with this strange result: the more invisible we have made death, the more we have come to fear it.

168
00:15:03.030 --> 00:15:05.880
Perhaps this is what al-Ghazali sensed.

169
00:15:05.880 --> 00:15:08.410
What you refuse to look at grows.

170
00:15:08.410 --> 00:15:13.340
So let us turn the question around, because that is where the real yield is.

171
00:15:13.340 --> 00:15:18.430
If death is bad and life is good, then living forever ought to be best of all.

172
00:15:18.430 --> 00:15:20.480
Is that really so?

173
00:15:20.480 --> 00:15:27.330
The British philosopher Bernard Williams approached this question in nineteen seventy-three by way of a work of literature.

174
00:15:27.330 --> 00:15:37.540
He discusses Elina Makropulos, the heroine of the play Věc Makropulos, The Makropulos Case, written by Karel Čapek and turned into an opera by Leoš Janáček.

175
00:15:37.540 --> 00:15:45.510
Elina's father is physician to an emperor, and he prepares an elixir that extends life; the elixir is first tested on his own daughter.

176
00:15:45.510 --> 00:15:48.200
The girl sees that it works.

177
00:15:48.200 --> 00:15:52.810
For more than three hundred years she has been forty-two years old.

178
00:15:52.810 --> 00:15:58.700
And what The Makropulos Case shows us is this: the woman is not happy.

179
00:15:58.700 --> 00:16:00.910
She is bored.

180
00:16:00.910 --> 00:16:07.280
She has seen everything, lived every love, known every betrayal, listened to every piece of music.

181
00:16:07.280 --> 00:16:11.090
People have become, for her, repeating types.

182
00:16:11.090 --> 00:16:17.940
Nothing promises her anything any more, because everything that could be promised has already happened and already worn out.

183
00:16:17.940 --> 00:16:21.430
In the end she refuses to renew the elixir.

184
00:16:21.430 --> 00:16:24.760
Williams draws a harsh conclusion from this.

185
00:16:24.760 --> 00:16:29.530
Our wanting to go on living depends, in fact, on particular desires.

186
00:16:29.530 --> 00:16:37.660
Finishing a book, seeing a child grow up, completing a piece of work, being reunited with someone, seeing a certain place.

187
00:16:37.660 --> 00:16:43.070
He calls these categorical desires: the things that get you out of bed tomorrow morning.

188
00:16:43.070 --> 00:16:48.000
In an endless life, all of these desires sooner or later run dry.

189
00:16:48.000 --> 00:16:50.850
Then two options remain.

190
00:16:50.850 --> 00:16:56.740
Either you stay the same person and life turns into an unending repetition, which is unbearable.

191
00:16:56.740 --> 00:17:05.830
Or you change so much over the centuries that the being who exists three hundred years from now is no longer you; this would not be the immortality of the you of today,

192
00:17:05.830 --> 00:17:09.480
but the life of a stranger who has taken your place.

193
00:17:09.480 --> 00:17:12.970
In neither case is there a winner.

194
00:17:12.970 --> 00:17:18.220
Now bring this back to your own life, because that is where the real question lies.

195
00:17:18.220 --> 00:17:22.350
If you knew you were going to live forever, what would you do today?

196
00:17:22.350 --> 00:17:26.320
For most of us, the honest answer is: nothing.

197
00:17:26.320 --> 00:17:28.690
You would put it off.

198
00:17:28.690 --> 00:17:38.900
You could write the book tomorrow just as easily, you could call your father next year, you could see that city a hundred years from now, you could drag the quarrel out for another century,

199
00:17:38.900 --> 00:17:42.550
you could save the apology for the next thousand years.

200
00:17:42.550 --> 00:17:47.800
Infinite time makes no choice meaningful, because no choice means giving anything up.

201
00:17:47.800 --> 00:17:52.890
Where choosing something costs you nothing, there is no such thing as a choice at all.

202
00:17:52.890 --> 00:17:56.540
An eighty-year life is about twenty-nine thousand days.

203
00:17:56.540 --> 00:18:01.630
Today is one of those days, and it is being spent right now, never to return.

204
00:18:01.630 --> 00:18:06.880
That sentence can be read as a threat, but it says exactly the opposite.

205
00:18:06.880 --> 00:18:10.530
The weight of everything you do comes precisely from this.

206
00:18:10.530 --> 00:18:15.460
The afternoon you give to someone is a gift, because you stole it from something else.

207
00:18:15.460 --> 00:18:21.190
The three months you spend keeping your anger alive are subtracted from a finite number of days.

208
00:18:21.190 --> 00:18:25.000
Being mortal is the very condition that makes life valuable.

209
00:18:25.000 --> 00:18:31.370
And now we can return to where we began, to that darkness before birth that never once costs you any sleep.

210
00:18:31.370 --> 00:18:35.020
Perhaps the symmetry argument teaches us nothing about death.

211
00:18:35.020 --> 00:18:39.630
Perhaps what it teaches us is how extraordinary it is to exist at all.

212
00:18:39.630 --> 00:18:46.480
Behind you lie billions of years that passed without you; ahead of you lie billions of years that will pass without you.

213
00:18:46.480 --> 00:18:57.970
Between these two immense stretches of darkness, an interval has opened up for reasons no one can fully explain, and inside that interval you are breathing, you are following these sentences, you love certain people,

214
00:18:57.970 --> 00:19:00.820
you are planning something for tomorrow.

215
00:19:00.820 --> 00:19:06.550
The real lesson of not grieving the nothingness before your birth is not that you should stop fearing death.

216
00:19:06.550 --> 00:19:12.760
It is this: the situation you find yourself in is not the rule, it is the exception.

217
00:19:12.760 --> 00:19:15.610
Epicurus did not leave this as a theory.

218
00:19:15.610 --> 00:19:25.660
According to Diogenes Laertius, on his deathbed, racked by unbearable stomach pain and the agony of kidney stones, he wrote a short letter to a friend named Idomeneus.

219
00:19:25.660 --> 00:19:29.150
He knew he was in the last day of his life.

220
00:19:29.150 --> 00:19:34.880
He wrote, too, that the pain in his body was at its very peak; he did not hide it.

221
00:19:34.880 --> 00:19:42.850
But against that he set the joy he took in the memory of the philosophical conversations they had shared, and he said the scales were balanced.

222
00:19:42.850 --> 00:19:46.980
He asked that his students be looked after, and he died that way.

223
00:19:46.980 --> 00:19:49.830
This is not the abolition of fear.

224
00:19:49.830 --> 00:19:53.320
It is fear balanced by memory and by friendship.

225
00:19:53.320 --> 00:19:58.250
And perhaps that is the most realistic answer we have.

226
00:19:58.250 --> 00:20:04.140
You do not grieve the nothingness before your birth, because there was no you there to be lost.

227
00:20:04.140 --> 00:20:07.150
The same will be true of what comes after death.

228
00:20:07.150 --> 00:20:11.440
The difference between them lies right now, in your hands.

229
00:20:11.440 --> 00:20:18.770
One darkness has passed, the other is coming, and in the interval of light between the two there is a great deal of work to be done.

230
00:20:18.770 --> 00:20:22.740
Nöron Philosophy'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

231
00:20:22.740 --> 00:20:26.710
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
